  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 445 AM EDT MON JUL 20 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, the area sits ahead of a \npotent cold front. This front is initially expected to extend\nacross the eastern Great Lakes to Lower OH Valley from a sfc low\nin Canad to the south and southeast of James Bay. Also at that\npoint, a shortwave trough is expected to be rotating through the\nGreat Lakes to OH and TN Valleys. \n\nA model sounding from the 00Z suite of data shows conditions at \n01Z Wednesday (9 PM Tuesday) as follows: MLCAPE around 2000 J/kg,\nEffective Bulk shear of 35-40 kts, 0-1 km SRH around 170 m2/s2 \nand 0-3 km SRH near 290 m2/s2, and DCAPE near 1300-1400 J/kg. \nDifferent models also suggest that PWAT could remain between 1.75 \nand 2.00 inches ahead of the cold frontal passage during the late\nevening and into the night. The above parameters highlight an \nenvironment favorable for organized cells to sustain updrafts \ngiven models sounding hodographs showing a critical angle of 92 \ndegrees (near textbook orthogonal flow). With DCAPE of 1300 J/kg, \nwet microbursts are also favored, where severe downburst winds of \n60-70 mph gusts or more could be possible. While low-level \nkinematics could still support discrete or semi-discrete storms \nnear sunset, the high DCAPE and potential strong cold pool \ndevelopment and forcing from the front suggest storms will likely \nconsolidate quite rapidly into a bowing QLCS/MCS by around or \nshortly after sunset. In summary, the greatest weather threat \nappears to be strong to severe damaging wind gusts, though \nisolated tornadoes and large hail around quarter-size cannot be \nruled out. As such, the SPC has put the area in a Day 2 Enhances \nRisk (Level 3 of 5) for Severe Weather, covering an area near and \nnorth of the Mountain Parkway with a Slight Risk (Level 2 of 5) \nfor Severe Weather elsewhere. \n\nTo add to a busy Tuesday evening mean PWATS of 1.75 to 2.00 inches \nacross the area fall within the 97th to 99th percentile against \nclimatology. Integrated water vapor transport will also approach the \n99th percentile of climatology during this timeframe. With days of \nrepetitive rainfall having already primed soils, any storms late\nin the evening into the overnight could produce additional periods\nof heavy or torrential rainfall which could lead to isolated to \nscattered instances of flash flooding. As such, the WPC has all of\nthe forecast area in a Slight Risk (Level 2 of 4) for Excessive \nRainfall meaning isolated to scattered instances of flash flooding\nwill remain possible into the evening. \n\nThe cold front is expected to be south of the forecast area by \nWednesday morning, though isolated to scattered showers and storms \nwill linger through the morning, with chances tapering by evening,\nas high pressure builds into the area. Quiet conditions are \nexpected across Eastern Kentucky from Wednesday night through the \nbetter half of Friday. However, models and ensembles depict the \nnext system ejecting out of the Central Plains later Friday and\nFriday night into the Tennessee Valley and then gradually across\nportions of the Southern Appalachians/Southeast. This scenario\nleads to renewed shower and storms chances Friday evening, \nthrough Sunday. At present, chances look to remain isolated to \nscattered in nature (15-35% chance), with the best chances further\nsouth nearer to the VA and TN borders. \n\nTemperatures will generally be 5 degrees below normal to start \nthe extended, ranging from the upper 70s to low 80s Wednesday and\nThursday following the cold frontal passage Tuesday night. \nTemperatures then generally warm to the mid to upper 80s for highs\nthis weekend. Overnight lows will range from the upper 60s to\naround 70 for Tuesday night, settled into the upper 50s to lower \n60s Wednesday and Thursday nights, and trend milder into the mid\nto upper 60s for most locations each night Friday though Sunday.",
